      simpleExplanation: "Before a Sukuk deal goes ahead, the people structuring it need to check many different kinds of risk - can the borrower actually pay, will the underlying asset hold its value, could exchange rates or early repayment throw off the numbers - and carefully study the contracts and expected cash flows involved.",
      academicExplanation: "Lead bankers undertake minute risk analysis covering: credit and bankruptcy risk (the entity's ability to pay obligations and survive as a viable entity); performance risk (ability to fulfil contractual obligations); asset/collateral risk (variation in underlying asset value); payment risk (ability of other parties, especially credit enhancement providers, to meet obligations); return rate risk (variation in return rate structure); exchange rate risk; liquidity risk (ability to liquidate the underlying asset/collateral to service investors); risk of loss on funds collected and retained briefly by the servicer before remittance to the SPV; prepayment risk (variation in investment maturity due to obligor prepayment); reinvestment risk in pay-through structures (variation in returns on SPV-held funds pending prespecified payment dates); and legal/regulatory/tax risk (interpretation of laws, regulations and complex documentation). Contract analysis focuses on rights and obligations, performance requirements, termination, events of default and consequences of default, including study of transaction documentation. Cash flow analysis identifies key variables and expected patterns of underlying cash flows under various scenarios to determine the rating. Box 15.2 summarizes how securitization mitigates risk: from the originator's perspective - mitigates liquidity risk of an illiquid asset, reduces FX risk if the underlying asset is multi-currency denominated, reduces cost of funding, takes assets off balance sheet without loss of use, and mitigates earnings risk via pooling of diversified/heterogeneous-risk assets (plus reduced finance cost if the SPV pools multiple originators); from the investors' perspective - undivided ownership of the asset provides added protection.",
