      title: "Chapter Summary and Prominent Sukuk Case Studies",
      overview: "The chapter closes by reiterating Sukuk's growth potential and credibility challenges, illustrated by real sale-and-lease-back sovereign and corporate Sukuk case studies from Dubai, Pakistan and Saudi Arabia.",
      simpleExplanation: "To bring the ideas together, the book walks through real examples - Dubai's giant Nakheel Sukuk, Pakistan's first sovereign Sukuk backed by a motorway, WAPDA's power-turbine Sukuk, and a Saudi vehicle-fleet securitization - showing how the theory of SPVs, asset transfer and sale-and-lease-back plays out in practice.",
      academicExplanation: "Sukuk offer tremendous growth potential for the global Islamic capital market, critical to the industry's sustained development, creating a framework for large-scale public/private-sector project participation (including infrastructure like roads, bridges, ports, airports). Sukuk issuance requires appropriate enabling laws protecting investors/issuers, accounting standards, targeted market study, standardized contract monitoring, appropriate financial data flow to investors, and standard-quality customer service. Islamic banks' credibility is fragile, especially in GCC states and Pakistan, making Shari'ah scholars' role crucial; international institutions (Bahrain-based LMC, IIFM, IIRA) need to do more to make Islamic capital markets active/efficient and to push industry players toward exploiting Shirkah-based Sukuk potential, since sole reliance on Ijarah Sukuk may not sustain the industry's full securitization potential. The text profiles four real transactions: (1) DP World's Nakheel Sukuk - initial offering $2.5 billion, final $3.52 billion, joint lead managers Barclays Capital and Dubai Islamic Bank, listed on Dubai International Financial Exchange, 3-year tenor, sale-and-lease-back (convertible) structure, priced at LIBOR+120bps, with ~38% Middle East/40% Europe/22% rest-of-world investor allocation and a security package including a land mortgage, share pledge and Dubai World parent guarantee. (2) Government of Pakistan's Ijarah Sukuk (Box 15.6) - Pakistan's first-ever Sukuk ($600 million, January 2005); Pakistan International Sukuk Company Limited (PIS), an SPV wholly owned by the government, bought M-2 motorway highway land from the National Highway Authority and issued trust certificates rated B+ by Standard & Poor's; the land was leased back to the government for the certificates' tenor, with periodic lease payments funding periodic Sukuk liabilities, and government repurchase of the land at maturity redeeming the Sukuk; the offer attracted $1,200 million in orders against $600 million accepted, priced at 220bps above six-month LIBOR. (3) WAPDA's Ijarah Sukuk (Box 15.7) - the Water and Power Development Authority financed enhanced power-generating capacity via local-currency Ijarah Sukuk; an SPV ('WAPDA First Sukuk Co', WFS) purchased ten power generation turbines at Mangla Hydel Power Station for lease-back to WAPDA over seven years, with rentals benchmarked to KIBOR (6-month KIBOR + 35bps) and semiannual payments; at lease-end, WAPDA repurchases the turbines per its unilateral undertaking; the government of Pakistan guarantees WAPDA's payment obligation, making the Sukuk eligible for maintaining Islamic banks' statutory liquidity requirement (SLR). (4) Hanco Fleet Securitization, Saudi Arabia (Box 15.8) - a $27.2 million issue, 6% periodic distribution, 3-year tenor, using a two-tier SPV/SPC structure (the SPC incorporated abroad due to stringent Saudi laws) to securitize a motor fleet: the SPC issues Sukuk and funds the SPV's asset purchase from the originator; the SPV owns the assets, uses an agent to manage them, and forwards cash flows to an offshore account managed by the SPC, which pays certificate holders and, at maturity, sells the assets to fund redemption.",
