      "id": "ch15-t11",
      "sectionNumber": "15.3.5",
      "sectionTitle": "Categories of Sukuk: Mudarabah (Muqaradah) Sukuk",
      "title": "Mudarabah Sukuk in Detail: The 1988 OIC Fiqh Council Rules",
      "overview": "Mudarabah (Muqaradah) Sukuk let the public participate in a Mudarabah-managed project, subject to a detailed seven-point rule set laid down by the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council in 1988 governing disclosure, sale and the prohibition on guarantees.",
      "simpleExplanation": "A Mudarabah Sukuk is a certificate showing you own a share of a specific project being run on a profit-sharing basis by a manager (the issuer). In 1988, Islamic scholars at the OIC laid down clear rules for these certificates: the prospectus must disclose everything about the deal, nobody can guarantee you a fixed return or your capital back, and how you can resell your certificate depends on whether the underlying money is still cash, has become a debt, or is a mix of assets.",
      "academicExplanation": "Mudarabah/Muqaradah Sukuk are certificates that represent projects or activities managed on the Mudarabah principle, with the issuer as Mudarib, subscribers as owners of capital, and the realized funds constituting the Mudarabah capital; certificate holders own the assets of the Mudarabah, are entitled to the agreed profit share, and bear any loss. Per the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council's fourth session (1988) resolution, the salient features are: (1) Mudarabah Sukuk (MS) represent common ownership and entitle holders to a share in the specific project against which they were issued; (2) the MS contract rests on the official notice/prospectus, which must disclose all Shari'ah-required Qirad information (nature of capital, profit-distribution ratio, and other Shari'ah-compatible conditions); (3) MS holders may sell their Sukuk in the securities market at their discretion, with the applicable sale rules depending on the form of the Mudarabah capital at the time - if still money, Bai' al Sarf rules apply (equal-for-equal, simultaneous, if homogeneous; simultaneous if heterogeneous); if in debt form, debt-trading rules apply; if a mixed combination of cash, receivables, goods, real assets and benefits, trading follows the market price by mutual consent; (4) the manager/SPV receiving subscriber funds may also invest its own funds, earning a separate capital-contribution profit in addition to its Mudarib share; (5) neither the prospectus nor the MS itself may contain a guarantee, from the issuer or fund manager, of the capital or of a fixed/percentage-based profit - so the prospectus/MS may not stipulate a specific payment amount, profit must be divided per Shari'ah rules as an amount in excess of capital (not mere revenue/yield), and the Mudarabah's Profit and Loss Account must be published and disseminated to holders; (6) it is permissible to create contingency reserves (e.g. against capital loss) by deducting a set percentage from profit each accounting period; (7) the prospectus may include a promise by a wholly unrelated third party (independent in legal entity and financial status) to donate a specific sum, without counter-benefit, to cover losses in a given project, provided the commitment is independent of the Mudarabah contract itself - though the issuer may never guarantee the Mudarabah capital. IFIs can offer Mudarabah Sukuk/certificates representing a bank's variable capital (class B share), marketed regionally through the sale of the issued Sukuk.",
      "examExplanation": "OIC Fiqh Council's 7-point Mudarabah Sukuk rules (1988): (1) common ownership in the specific project; (2) prospectus must disclose all Qirad-required information; (3) holder's resale rules depend on capital form - cash (Bai' al Sarf), debt (debt-trading rules), mixed (market price by consent); (4) manager/SPV may also invest own funds, earning separately from its Mudarib share; (5) NO guarantee of capital or fixed/percentage profit by issuer/manager - profit distributed as excess over capital, P&L published; (6) contingency reserves permitted via profit deduction; (7) an unrelated third party MAY promise a free-of-charge loss-covering donation, but the issuer itself may never guarantee the capital.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Mudarabah Sukuk holders own the project's assets, share agreed profit, and bear any loss, with the issuer acting as Mudarib",
        "Resale rules for MS depend on the form of the Mudarabah capital at the time: cash (Bai' al Sarf rules), debt (debt-trading rules), or mixed (market price by mutual consent)",
        "The manager/SPV may invest its own funds alongside subscribers' funds, earning a separate capital-contribution profit on top of its Mudarib share",
        "Neither the prospectus nor the MS itself may guarantee capital or a fixed/percentage-based profit to holders - profit must be an amount in excess of capital, determined per Shari'ah rules, with the P&L Account published to holders",
        "Contingency reserves against capital loss may be created by deducting a set percentage from profit each accounting period",
        "An unrelated third party may promise a free, independent donation to cover project losses, but the issuer itself can never guarantee the Mudarabah capital"
      ],
