      "sectionTitle": "Categories of Sukuk: Musharakah Sukuk",
      "title": "Musharakah Sukuk: Structure, Tradability and Sudan's Central Bank/Government Certificates",
      "overview": "Musharakah Sukuk mirror Mudarabah Sukuk's rules but make the intermediary a genuine loss-sharing partner, making them the more equitable structure - illustrated by Sudan's real-world use of Musharakah certificates for open market operations.",
      "simpleExplanation": "A Musharakah Sukuk works much like a Mudarabah Sukuk, except the manager is a real partner who shares in both the ups and downs, not just the profits - which is why scholars consider it fairer. Sudan actually uses these certificates instead of interest-bearing treasury bills for the central bank's everyday operations.",
      "academicExplanation": "Musharakah is a mode that can serve easily as a basis for securitization, especially for big projects requiring huge amounts. Every subscriber is given a Musharakah certificate representing proportionate ownership in the project's assets; these are certificates of equal value issued to mobilize funds on a partnership basis, so holders become owners of the relevant project/asset as per their respective shares. Musharakah Sukuk can be issued as redeemable certificates by or to the corporate sector or individuals - for rehabilitation/employment, automobile purchase for commercial use, or establishment of clinics, hospitals, factories, trading centres, endowments, etc. Musharakah redeemable Sukuk are almost identical to Mudarabah Sukuk in their basic Shari'ah rules, with one major difference: the intermediary party is a partner of the group of subscribers (represented by the body of Musharakah certificate holders), in a manner similar to a joint stock company. Once a project starts, Musharakah certificates can be treated as negotiable instruments; certificates based on Musharakah/Mudarabah can be bought/sold in the secondary market subject to the condition that the portfolio comprises nonliquid assets valuing more than 50%; profit is shared per an agreed ratio and loss on a pro rata basis; where there is a combination of liquid and nonliquid assets, the combination can be sold/purchased for an amount greater than the amount of liquid assets in the pool. Investment Sukuk can be issued on a Musharakah basis to mobilize short-term deposits for developing long-term projects, or for general financial/specific-project investment; proceeds can buy and lease equipment, or fund construction, expansion or working capital. The Musharakah structure is considered more equitable and safer for investors than the Mudarabah structure, since it involves both profit- and loss-sharing between fund manager and Sukuk holders (not only profit-sharing), giving holders added comfort/security from the manager's own capital participation cushion. In Sudan, a number of assets of the Ministry of Finance, the Bank of Sudan, Bank of Khartoum, Nilain Bank and other public entities have been identified for securitization on a Musharakah basis; central bank Musharakah certificates (CMCs) and government Musharakah certificates (GMCs) have been issued since 1998 as investor instruments, used in place of treasury bills and other interest-bearing securities for open market operations and monetary management by the central bank - CMCs are sold/bought by the central bank through auctions and can be traded in the secondary inter-bank market.",
      "examExplanation": "Musharakah Sukuk = proportionate project/asset ownership certificate; basic rules mirror Mudarabah Sukuk EXCEPT the intermediary is a genuine PARTNER (profit AND loss sharing, like a joint stock company) - considered more equitable/safer than Mudarabah (profit-sharing only by manager). Tradable once project starts, if nonliquid assets >50% of portfolio; profit per agreed ratio, loss pro rata. Sudan since 1998: CMCs (central bank Musharakah certificates) and GMCs (government Musharakah certificates) replace treasury bills for open market operations; CMCs sold/bought via central bank auctions, tradable in the secondary inter-bank market.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Musharakah Sukuk mirror Mudarabah Sukuk's basic Shari'ah rules, except the intermediary is a genuine partner among the certificate holders, similar to a joint stock company",
        "Musharakah is considered more equitable and safer for investors than Mudarabah because the fund manager shares both profit AND loss, not just profit",
        "Musharakah/Mudarabah certificates become negotiable once the project starts, tradable in the secondary market only if nonliquid assets exceed 50% of the portfolio",
        "Musharakah Sukuk proceeds can fund equipment purchase/leasing, construction, project expansion, or working capital for long-term projects",
        "Sudan has issued central bank Musharakah certificates (CMCs) and government Musharakah certificates (GMCs) since 1998, replacing treasury bills for open market operations and monetary management",
        "CMCs are sold/bought by Sudan's central bank through auctions and can be traded in the secondary inter-bank market"
      ],
