      "overview": "Salam, Istisna'a and Murabaha Sukuk each securitize a different underlying debt-creating transaction with distinct tradability limits, while mixed portfolio Sukuk combine several modes to diversify risk and return, illustrated by the IDB's Solidarity Trust Sukuk.",
      "simpleExplanation": "Salam Sukuk let a bank raise cash now by promising to deliver a commodity later (like aluminium), Istisna'a Sukuk fund something being custom-built, and Murabaha Sukuk fund a cost-plus credit sale - all three represent a debt owed to the certificate holders, so none of them can normally be freely resold at a discount, only redeemed at face value or transferred once the debt turns into an owned asset. Mixed portfolio Sukuk blend several of these techniques together, like the IDB's landmark $400 million issue that combined leasing, cost-plus sales and manufacturing contracts in one pool.",
      "academicExplanation": "Salam is a contract in which the price is paid in advance for goods to be delivered later; per the AAOIFI Standard, a Salam purchaser can onward-sell the commodity via an independent Parallel Salam contract (specifications/delivery dates may conform between the two, but each remains independently enforceable). Salam Sukuk are certificates of equal value issued to mobilize capital paid in advance as the price of a commodity to be delivered later; the Salam commodity's seller issues the certificates, and subscribers are the buyers/eventual owners of the commodity; under Parallel Salam, Sukuk holders are entitled to the commodity or its selling price at delivery. Salam sale benefits the seller (enhanced advance cash flow) and buyer (a Salam price normally below the prevailing spot price); the Bahrain Monetary Agency (BMA), in June 2001, developed LIBOR-related three-month Salam-based securities used by Islamic banks for maintaining statutory liquidity (SLR) - the Bahrain government sells aluminium to Bahrain Islamic Bank (BIB, representing other participating banks) under Salam for future delivery, while BIB appoints the government its agent to market the aluminium at delivery through its distribution channels at a price providing security holders a return; similar short-term Sukuk could be developed for heavily-traded commodities like crude oil and cotton. Secondary-market trading of Salam Sukuk is generally considered impermissible, since the certificates represent a share in Salam debt subject to debt-trading rules (the issue needing further analysis regarding reselling Salam goods before the original buyer takes possession, especially where the bank maintains fungible inventory). Istisna'a is a manufacturing contract allowing cash-in-advance/future-delivery or future-payment/future-delivery structures, usable for financing houses, plant, projects, bridges, roads and highways; via a Parallel Istisna'a with subcontractors, Islamic banks can undertake a project's construction and its deferred-price sale while subcontracting actual construction to specialized firms. In Istisna'a, full ownership of the constructed item transfers immediately upon delivery against the deferred sale price (which legitimately covers construction cost, profit, and the cost of tying up funds over the repayment period); this deferred price can be documented as Istisna'a Sukuk (certificates of indebtedness) to mobilize the funds needed to produce the item, with the manufacturer/seller as issuer and the item's eventual buyers as subscribers/holders. Istisna'a certificates can be traded/redeemed only if the mobilized funds have converted through business/trade into assets owned by certificate holders during the Istisna'a operation; if realized funds are immediately paid as the price in a Parallel Istisna'a, or the manufactured item is submitted to the ultimate purchaser, trading follows debt-disposal rules (the Sukuk then representing a monetary debt owed by the ultimate purchaser), meaning such certificates can only be cashed at maturity/face value (though transferable at face value to a third party) - the prohibition of Riba precludes selling them at any other price. On securitization via Murabaha: any paper representing a monetary right/obligation from a credit-sale transaction cannot create a negotiable Sukuk instrument, since the purchaser's note evidences a debt receivable that must be transferred at par value under Hawalah rules; however, if a commodity has been purchased but not yet sold, certificates against it are tradable since they represent a tradable asset. A mixed portfolio including Murabaha may issue negotiable certificates if Ijarah/other fixed assets exceed 50% of total worth (or, under the Hanafi view, if nonliquid assets exceed just 10%). Murabaha Sukuk are especially likely for public-sector purchases of high-value items paid in instalments; the government issues certificates per instalment, each representing a transferable property right of the seller (provided the claim amount doesn't change), and the original certificate holder can transfer collection rights subject to recourse equal to face value minus the transferee's collection cost. Arcapita Bank B.S.C (Bahrain) issued five-year multicurrency Murabaha-backed Sukuk in 2005 (bullet maturity, proceeds used for a series of commodity Murabaha sale/purchase transactions), offering three-month LIBOR + 175bps, with full SPV recourse to Arcapita, making the Sukuk freely transferable given the SPV's presumed sufficient inventory/fixed assets. Mixed Portfolio Sukuk securitize a pool of Musharakah, Ijarah and some Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a and Ju'alah contracts, with return/risk depending on the chosen mix; the IDB's $400 million Solidarity Trust Sukuk (2003) is the prominent example: Solidarity Trust Services (STS), as trustee, issued fixed-rate trust certificates funding a pool of Ijarah, Murabaha and Istisna'a contracts originated by IDB, each certificate ranking pari passu; Ijarah assets had to exceed 50% of the pool at all times, with a dissolution event (triggering IDB's obligation to purchase all trust assets per a separate 'purchase undertaking deed') if Ijarah assets fell below 25%; profit net of trust expenses funded periodic returns, certificates were redeemed at 100% of principal value, and principal amounts were reinvested in Ijarah and Musharakah contracts. The modus operandi of mixed portfolio Sukuk is an effective tool for converting nonmarketable/illiquid assets into negotiable instruments with a secondary market, particularly suited to investment banks and DFIs.",
      "examExplanation": "Salam Sukuk: advance-payment cash mobilization for future commodity delivery; seller issues, subscribers are buyers; NOT tradable secondary market (debt-trading rules), except via Parallel Salam entitlement; BMA's 2001 LIBOR-linked aluminium Salam securities example. Istisna'a Sukuk: document deferred Istisna'a price; tradable only if funds converted to owned assets; otherwise face-value-only debt certificate. Murabaha Sukuk: receivables alone can't be securitized (Hawalah, par value only); tradable if goods purchased-but-unsold, or in a mixed pool with >50% Ijarah/fixed assets (Hanafi: >10% nonliquid). Mixed Portfolio Sukuk: combine Musharakah/Ijarah/Murabaha/Salam/Istisna'a/Ju'alah; IDB Solidarity Trust Sukuk = landmark example, >50% Ijarah required, dissolution trigger <25% Ijarah.",
